Tasks: Domain model (OrderItem/NewOrderItem), transactional store (OrderStore.create accepts items, computes totalCents, atomic insert via Skunk transaction; get/update return items too), defense-in-depth DB CHECK constraints (V5), and full HTTP layer wiring (CreateOrderItemRequest/OrderItemResponse, EmptyOrderItems/InvalidOrderItem errors, item validation, updated server logic)

Summary: POST /orders now accepts {customerId, items: [...]} instead of {customerId, totalCents} - totalCents is computed server-side as sum(quantity * unitPriceCents). Order + all its OrderItems are persisted atomically in one Postgres transaction (session.transaction.use wrapping both inserts) - verified by a test that an invalid second item (quantity -1, now rejected by a new V5 CHECK constraint) rolls back the whole insert, leaving no order row behind. GET/PATCH/PUT responses now include the order's items. Empty items list or an item with quantity<=0/unitPriceCents<0 is rejected with 400 before touching the store, via two new OrderError cases (EmptyOrderItems, InvalidOrderItem) added to the existing oneOf-based errorOut wiring. DELETE's existing FK CASCADE still removes order_items (tested end-to-end via HTTP in OrderRoutesSuite, and store-level in OrderStorePostgresSuite).

Combined what was planned as two separate phases (store layer, then HTTP layer) into one commit - documented as a deviation in plan.md - because OrderStore.create's signature change breaks OrderRoutes.scala's call site immediately; Scala's whole-module compilation means there's no way to reach a green/compiling state after the store layer alone, unlike the plan's original premise that it could be verified independently first.

Files: OrderError.scala (2 new cases), OrderStore.scala (domain types + transactional create/get/update), OrderRoutes.scala (request/response shapes, errorOut wiring, server logic), V5__add_order_items_checks.sql (new), HealthRoutesSuite/OrderDocsSuite/OrderRoutesSuite/OrderStorePostgresSuite/OrderStoreSuite (updated/extended for new signatures and 8 new behavioral tests)

Why: US-3.1 - checkout creates an order, the core of the walking-skeleton's Phase 1 (per gluon/PLAN.md), closing out the Scala domain/store layer the two prior order_items tracks (table + FK) deliberately deferred to this track.
